// A type with several constructors licenses no η cell, and the route says so
// rather than guessing one.
//
// `pred(Succ(m)) ==> m` looks like the inverse face an η law is licensed by,
// and for `Succ` it is one. But `NatPred` has two constructors, so the law
// `Succ(pred(w)) = w` is false at `Zero`: `pred(Zero)` is not written here at
// all, and even written it could not make `Succ` of it equal `Zero`. An η law
// cancels a destructor against *the* constructor it inverts, and a type with
// two has no such constructor.
//
// So the cell layer declines, and the decline names the half that is missing
// with the count that decides it. Licensing no η law is the ordinary case, not
// a failure — most declarations are in it — so the reason is reported where a
// reader can ask for it rather than raised as a diagnostic.
//
// The rest of the declaration is unaffected: the two constructor frames and
// the written rule still reach the store.
data NatPred : Type {
  Zero : NatPred;
  Succ : (n : NatPred) --> NatPred;
  oper pred(n : NatPred) -> NatPred;
  rule pred(Succ(m)) ==> m;
}

//@ mode: desc
//@ expect-desc-rules: 1
//@ expect-desc-store-cells: 3
//@ expect-desc-eta-cells: 0
//@ expect-desc-no-eta: the declaration has 2 constructors
